</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define a Type II error.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define test power.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define a <m>p</m>-value.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why a <m>p</m>-value is not the probability that <m>H_0</m> is true.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define statistical significance.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>\overline{x}=103,
\qquad
\mu_0=100,
\qquad
\sigma=12,
\qquad
n=64.</md>
</p>

<p>Compute the one-sample <m>z</m>-test statistic.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>For the previous problem, write the two-sided <m>p</m>-value using
<m>\Phi</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>n=36,
\qquad
\overline{x}=54,
\qquad
s=12.</md>
</p>

<p>Test</p>

<p>
<md>H_0:\mu=50.</md>
</p>

<p>Compute the <m>t</m>-statistic and degrees of freedom.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>n=100,
\qquad
x=62.</md>
</p>

<p>Test</p>

<p>
<md>H_0:p=0.50.</md>
</p>

<p>Compute the one-proportion <m>z</m>-statistic.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>For two samples,</p>

<p>
<md>\overline{x}_1=20,\quad s_1=5,\quad n_1=30,</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\overline{x}_2=17,\quad s_2=6,\quad n_2=40,</md>
</p>

<p>compute the Welch test statistic for</p>

<p>
<md>H_0:\mu_1=\mu_2.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Suppose paired differences satisfy</p>

<p>
<md>\overline{d}=2,
\qquad
s_D=3,
\qquad
n=25.</md>
</p>

<p>Compute the paired <m>t</m>-statistic for</p>

<p>
<md>H_0:\mu_D=0.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>x_1=80,\ n_1=100,
\qquad
x_2=60,\ n_2=100.</md>
</p>

<p>Compute the pooled proportion under</p>

<p>
<md>H_0:p_1=p_2.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Use the pooled proportion from the previous problem to write the
two-proportion <m>z</m>-statistic.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Suppose a normal sample has</p>

<p>
<md>n=20,
\qquad
s^2=16.</md>
</p>

<p>Write the chi-square statistic for testing</p>

<p>
<md>H_0:\sigma^2=9.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>A contingency table cell has row total <m>70</m>, column total <m>60</m>, and
grand total <m>210</m>. Find its expected count under independence.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Suppose six categories are completely specified under a
goodness-of-fit null. Find the usual degrees of freedom.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>If <m>20</m> tests are Bonferroni-adjusted to control FWER at <m>0.05</m>,
find the individual significance level.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive the one-sample <m>z</m>-test rejection region for a two-sided
alternative.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive the one-proportion null standard error.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why the one-proportion hypothesis test uses <m>p_0</m> in the
standard error while a Wald confidence interval uses <m>\widehat{p}</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive the Welch standard error for two independent means.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive the pooled two-sample <m>t</m>-statistic under equal normal
variances.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why paired data can have a much smaller standard error than an
independent-groups analysis.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive the two-proportion pooled standard error under
<m>H_0:p_1=p_2</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive the chi-square statistic for testing a normal variance.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive the <m>F</m>-statistic for comparing two normal population
variances.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive the expected-count formula</p>

<p>
<md>E_{ij}
=
\frac{
n_{i\cdot}n_{\cdot j}
}{n}</md>
</p>

<p>under independence.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why a goodness-of-fit test with <m>k</m> fully specified categories
has <m>k-1</m> degrees of freedom.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain how estimating parameters from the same sample can reduce
goodness-of-fit degrees of freedom.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive the likelihood-ratio statistic</p>

<p>
<md>-2\log\Lambda.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Compare Wald, score, and likelihood-ratio tests conceptually.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>State the Neyman--Pearson lemma and explain its optimality criterion.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive the power function for a one-sided known-variance normal mean
test.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why power increases with sample size.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why increasing <m>\alpha</m> increases both power and Type I error
risk.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive the sample-size planning formula for a one-sided <m>z</m>-test.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why failure to reject <m>H_0</m> is not equivalent to evidence for
